# Artificial reporter gene providing MRI contrast based on proton exchange

## abstract
Existing magnetic resonance reporter genes all rely on the presence of (super)paramagnetic substances and employ water relaxation to gain contrast. We designed a nonmetallic, biodegradable, lysine rich-protein (LRP) reporter, the prototype of a potential family of genetically engineered reporters expressing artificial proteins with frequency-selective contrast. This endogenous contrast, based on transfer of radiofrequency labeling from the reporter's amide protons to water protons, can be switched on and off.

B R I E F C O M M U N I C A T I O N S
Assaf A Gilad1,2, Michael T McMahon1,3, Piotr Walczak1,2, Paul T Winnard $\mathbf{Jr^{1}}$ , Venu Raman1,4, Hanneke W M van Laarhoven1,2,5, Cynthia M Skoglund1,2, Jeff W M Bulte1,2 & Peter C M van Zijl1,3
Existing magnetic resonance reporter genes all rely on the presence of (super)paramagnetic substances and employ water relaxation to gain contrast. We designed a nonmetallic, biodegradable, lysine rich–protein (LRP) reporter, the prototype of a potential family of genetically engineered reporters expressing artificial proteins with frequency-selective contrast. This endogenous contrast, based on transfer of radiofrequency labeling from the reporter’s amide protons to water protons, can be switched on and off.
The sensitivity of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) to (super)- paramagnetic metals has allowed visualization of gene expression1,2 and high-resolution tracking of transplanted cells3,4. Except for recently reported endogenous reporter genes5,6, which generate contrast themselves by accumulating MR-detectable iron, contrast materials must be administered and their concentration is lowered with every cell division, thus reducing detection efficiency. Also, the metalinduced contrast mechanism allows detection of only one type of labeled cell. Recently, a new type of contrast was suggested in which radiofrequency irradiation is used to noninvasively label agent protons that exchange with water7–11. The contrast produced by these so-called chemical-exchange saturation transfer (CEST)7 agents, unlike that produced by (super)paramagnetic metals, can be switched on and off by selectively irradiating (saturating) at the exchangeable protonresonance frequency (Fig. 1a). Because rapidly exchanging amide protons in polypeptides such as poly-L-lysine8 give CEST contrast in solution, we sought to design a reporter gene that encodes an LRP. The goal was to create contrast material that is detectable in the micromolar range, is biodegradable, can distinguish viable from nonviable cells and enables a constant endogenous level of expression even after multiple cell divisions.
We inoculated LRP-expressing (pooled clones) and EGFPexpressing (control) xenografts in opposite hemispheres of nonobese diabetic/severe combined immunodeficiency mouse brains. The signal intensity–difference map $\Delta\omega=\pm3.76$ p.p.m.) could clearly distinguish LRP-expressing tumors from control tumors (Fig. 3b). The signal-intensity change for LRP $\cdot8.2\%\pm3.2\%>$ brain baseline) was significantly different ( $(P=0.03^{\cdot}$ ) Fig. 3c) from control $(3.5\%\pm$ $3.3\%$ ), confirming LRP as a potential reporter for tracking cells. Xenografted cells proliferated and formed a brain tumor that appeared homogeneous in eosin-hematoxylin staining (Figs. 3e–g). Notably, xenografts dissected from rat brain $10\mathrm{~d~}$ after inoculation showed transgenic cells still expressing lrp mRNA (Fig. 3d).
Several experimental issues are important for CEST imaging. Most important is proper centering of the water resonance in each pixel to accurately determine the $\Delta\omega~=~\pm3.76$ p.p.m. signal-intensity difference, which depends on sufficient magnetic field homogeneity. Also, CEST contrast improves at higher fields. We used a field strength of $11.7\ \mathrm{T},$ but expect the approach to be extendable to clinical machines (3T). Recently, using fields of $4.7\ \mathrm{T}^{12,13}$ and $3\ \mathrm{T}^{14}$ , we were able to detect endogenous proteins in brain12 and tumor cells13,14. In xenografted 9L glioma cells, the CEST signal-intensity difference occurred over a broad frequency range (2–4 p.p.m. from water)13. Fortunately, the CEST effect from LRP is more frequency selective than that of the total amide proton pool in the target cells and therefore distinguishable. In addition, CEST contrast directly depends on both the total concentration of amide protons and their exchange rate. For $22\ \mathrm{kDa}$ poly-L-lysine, an amide proton exchange rate of $403\ s^{-1}$ was measured at $\mathrm{pH}=7.3$ (ref. 15), in contrast to a rate of $28\ s^{-1}$ for combined amide protons of multiple endogenous proteins in situ in rat brain12. The availability of MRI methods for measuring amide proton exchange rates through the water signal15 will allow determination of the presence of the contrast agent. Another feature of LRP is that amide proton exchange is base catalyzed and thus strongly pH dependent (the rate is reduced by a factor of $\sim10$ per $\mathrm{\pH}$ unit)15. Thus, contrast is lowered by an order of magnitude during ischemia or cell death12, whereas (super)paramagnetic agents may persist in dead cells for weeks to months.
For all reporter genes to date, a naturally occurring protein is selected and given a new application to report cellular activity or localization. LRP, however, was designed de novo based on desired properties (that is, a polypeptide with a high amide proton exchange rate). It is a prototype for CEST-based reporter genes. Future reporter targets with reduced charge and increased exchange rate should allow increased detection sensitivity. Additional frequency-specific reporter genes may be designed to include imino, amine and guanidine protons, giving the potential of multiple labeling. We expect the concept of CEST reporter genes to have applications in noninvasive cell tracking and the study of gene delivery and gene expression.
